#: ../../../api/generated/pygmt.project.rst:2
msgid "pygmt.project"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pygmt/src/project.py:docstring
#: of pygmt.src.project.project:1
msgid "Project data onto lines or great circles, or generate tracks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pygmt/src/project.py:docstring
#: of pygmt.src.project.project:3
msgid ""
"Project reads arbitrary :math:`(x, y [, z])` data and returns any "
"combination of :math:`(x, y, z, p, q, r, s)`, where :math:`(p, q)` are the "
"coordinates in the projection, :math:`(r, s)` is the position in the :math:"
"`(x, y)` coordinate system of the point on the profile (:math:`q = 0` path) "
"closest to :math:`(x, y)`, and :math:`z` is all remaining columns in the "
"input (beyond the required :math:`x` and :math:`y` columns)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pygmt/src/project.py:docstring
#: of pygmt.src.project.project:10
msgid ""
"Alternatively, ``project`` may be used to generate :math:`(r, s, p)` "
"triplets at equal increments along a profile using the ``generate`` "
"parameter. In this case, the value of ``data`` is ignored (you can use, e."
"g., ``data=None``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pygmt/src/project.py:docstring
#: of pygmt.src.project.project:15
msgid "Projections are defined in any (but only) one of three ways:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pygmt/src/project.py:docstring
#: of pygmt.src.project.project:17
msgid "By a ``center`` and an ``azimuth`` in degrees clockwise from North."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pygmt/src/project.py:docstring
#: of pygmt.src.project.project:18
msgid "By a ``center`` and ``endpoint`` of the projection path."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pygmt/src/project.py:docstring
#: of pygmt.src.project.project:19
msgid "By a ``center`` and a ``pole`` position."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pygmt/src/project.py:docstring
#: of pygmt.src.project.project:21
msgid ""
"To spherically project data along a great circle path, an oblique coordinate "
"system is created which has its equator along that path, and the zero "
"meridian through the Center. Then the oblique longitude (:math:`p`) "
"corresponds to the distance from the Center along the great circle, and the "
"oblique latitude (:math:`q`) corresponds to the distance perpendicular to "
"the great circle path. When moving in the increasing (:math:`p`) direction, "
"(toward B or in the azimuth direction), the positive (:math:`q`) direction "
"is to your left. If a Pole has been specified, then the positive (:math:`q`) "
"direction is toward the pole."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pygmt/src/project.py:docstring
#: of pygmt.src.project.project:31
msgid ""
"To specify an oblique projection, use the ``pole`` parameter to set the "
"pole. Then the equator of the projection is already determined and the "
"``center`` parameter is used to locate the :math:`p = 0` meridian. The "
"center *cx/cy* will be taken as a point through which the :math:`p = 0` "
"meridian passes. If you do not care to choose a particular point, use the "
"South pole (*cx* = 0, *cy* = -90)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pygmt/src/project.py:docstring
#: of pygmt.src.project.project:38
msgid ""
"Data can be selectively windowed by using the ``length`` and ``width`` "
"parameters. If ``width`` is used, the projection width is set to use only "
"data with :math:`w_{min} < q < w_{max}`. If ``length`` is set, then the "
"length is set to use only those data with :math:`l_{min} < p < l_{max}`. If "
"the ``endpoint`` parameter has been used to define the projection, then "
"``length=\"w\"`` may be used to window the length of the projection to "
"exactly the span from O to B."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pygmt/src/project.py:docstring
#: of pygmt.src.project.project:46
msgid ""
"Flat Earth (Cartesian) coordinate transformations can also be made. Set "
"``flat_earth=True`` and remember that azimuth is clockwise from North (the y-"
"axis), NOT the usual cartesian theta, which is counterclockwise from the x-"
"axis. azimuth = 90 - theta."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pygmt/src/project.py:docstring
#: of pygmt.src.project.project:51
msgid ""
"No assumptions are made regarding the units for :math:`x, y, r, s, p, q, "
"dist, l_{min}, l_{max}, w_{min}, w_{max}`. If ``unit`` is selected, map "
"units are assumed and :math:`x, y, r, s` must be in degrees and :math:`p, q, "
"dist, l_{min}, l_{max}, w_{min}, w_{max}` will be in km."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../.conda/lib/python3.14/site-packages/pygmt/src/project.py:docstring
#: of pygmt.src.project.project:58
msgid ""
"Calculations of specific great-circle and geodesic distances or for back-"
"azimuths or azimuths are better done using :gmt-docs:`mapproject` as project "
"is strictly spherical."
msgstr ""

